# Chapter 10 · Nature versus Culture: A Debate in Gender Development

> The syllabus calls it "Nature versus Culture: A Debate in Gender Development". The examiner asked it in 2018, joined to the sex/gender debate ("reflecting the nature versus nurture argument"). You need both sides with evidence, a famous case study, the anthropological framing, and a reasoned conclusion.

## 10.1  The debate stated

::: define Nature versus culture (nurture) | the central debate of gender development
The debate over whether the differences between women and men in behaviour, abilities and roles come mainly from **nature** (genes, hormones, brain structure, evolution) or mainly from **culture** (upbringing, socialisation, social structures and expectations).
-> The debate matters because it decides whether gender inequality is fixed or changeable.
:::

"Gender development" means the process by which a child comes to have a gender identity, gendered behaviour and gendered roles. The question is what drives that process.

## 10.2  The case for nature

1. **Chromosomes and hormones.** Sex chromosomes (XX, XY) direct the development of the reproductive system; hormones — especially **testosterone** before birth and at puberty — influence physical development and, many researchers argue, some behaviour.
2. **Evidence from hormonal conditions.** Girls with **congenital adrenal hyperplasia** (CAH), who are exposed to high levels of male hormones before birth, show on average a greater preference for toys usually favoured by boys (studies by Sheri Berenbaum and Melissa Hines, 1992 onwards).
3. **Average differences.** Across many societies, men are on average more physically aggressive and physically stronger; some studies report small average differences in certain spatial or verbal tasks.
4. **Evolutionary psychology and sociobiology.** Scholars such as **E. O. Wilson** (*Sociobiology*, ==1975==) and **David Buss** argue that evolution shaped different reproductive strategies in men and women, which still influence behaviour.
5. **Biosocial theories of crime.** The **evolutionary neuroandrogenic theory** (Lee Ellis) argues that evolution and male hormones (androgens) explain why men commit more violent crime in every society — the "belief that gender differences are universal" asked in a 2018 MCQ.

## 10.3  The case for culture

1. **Cross-cultural variation.** Margaret Mead's three New Guinea societies (==1935==) showed "masculine" and "feminine" temperaments distributed very differently (Chapter 6).
2. **Historical change.** In the World Wars women did "men's work" in factories and fields; in two generations women in many countries moved from exclusion to majority enrolment in universities. Biology did not change in that time; society did.
3. **Adults treat babies differently.** In "Baby X" experiments of the 1970s (for example Condry and Condry, ==1976==), adults shown the same baby described the same behaviour differently — as "anger" if told it was a boy, "fear" if told it was a girl.
4. **Socialisation is everywhere.** From pink and blue clothes to toys, chores, school subjects and television, children receive constant messages about gender (Chapter 6).
5. **The brain is plastic.** **Cordelia Fine** (*Delusions of Gender*, ==2010==) argued that many claimed brain differences are weak or poorly measured, and that the brain changes with experience. **Daphna Joel** (==2015==) found that most individual brains are a "mosaic" of features, not neatly "male" or "female".

::: case The David Reimer case | the limits of "pure nurture"
In 1966 a Canadian baby boy, David Reimer, lost his penis in a botched circumcision. On the advice of the psychologist John Money, he was surgically altered and raised as a girl. Money reported the case as proof that gender identity was learnt. In fact Reimer never felt himself to be a girl; he reverted to living as a male in his teens, and later spoke publicly about the harm done to him. He died in 2004.
-> The case warns against both extremes: gender identity was not simply created by upbringing — and neither doctors nor theories should impose a gender on a person.
:::

## 10.4  The anthropological framing: nature and culture as a gendered pair

::: thinker Sherry Ortner | American anthropologist · "Is Female to Male as Nature Is to Culture?" (1974)
Ortner asked why women are devalued in almost every society. Her answer: every culture sees itself as rising above nature, and women — because of their bodies (menstruation, childbirth, breastfeeding) and their confinement to the domestic sphere — are seen as closer to nature, while men are associated with culture. Women are then ranked lower, as nature is ranked below culture.
-> The very pairing of women with "nature" and men with "culture" is a cultural idea — which means the "nature" side of the debate is itself partly culture.
:::

## 10.5  The resolution: interactionism

Almost all scholars today reject the extremes. The **interactionist** (or biosocial) view holds that:

- biology provides **potentials and predispositions**, not destinies;
- culture **shapes, magnifies, reduces or reverses** those predispositions;
- the two interact throughout life — hormones affect behaviour, and behaviour and experience affect hormones and brains.

::: fig spectrum | Positions in the nature–culture debate.
@left Nature
@right Culture
8: Biological determinism
28: Sociobiology and evolutionary psychology
50: Interactionism (the modern consensus)
72: Social learning and socialisation theories
92: Strong social constructionism
:::

::: simple
Nature deals the cards; culture decides the rules of the game. Two players with similar cards can end very differently depending on the rules — and the rules, unlike the cards, can be changed.
:::

## 10.6  Why the debate matters — especially for policy

::: table | What each side implies for policy.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
If … | It implies … | The danger
Differences are mostly natural | Unequal roles are inevitable; policy should accept "natural" divisions | Justifies discrimination: "women are not suited to leadership, science or the army"
Differences are mostly cultural | Education, law and social change can remove unfair differences | Can ignore real biological needs (pregnancy, maternal health, safety)
Nature and culture interact (the evidence) | Remove social barriers **and** meet biological needs | —
:::

::: pakistan | The evidence from Pakistan
Pakistan's own experience supports the cultural and interactionist views. Women's literacy has risen from very low levels at independence to about ==53%== (Census 2023); girls frequently top the board examinations; women now serve as fighter pilots, judges of the Supreme Court, police officers and chief minister. None of these changes involved a change in biology. They followed changes in education, law, opportunity and attitudes — and they show how much of the "natural" order was social all along.
:::

::: eye | The 2018 essay: sex versus gender, reflecting nature versus nurture
Structure it as: (1) introduction — define sex, gender, nature and nurture; (2) the sex/gender distinction (Stoller, Oakley); (3) the nature argument, with evidence; (4) the nurture argument, with evidence (Mead, Baby X, Fine); (5) the David Reimer case — the limits of both extremes; (6) queer theory's challenge — is sex constructed too? (Chapter 8); (7) Ortner's framing; (8) interactionism; (9) policy implications and Pakistan; (10) conclusion. Draw the spectrum.
:::
